SOURCE @agaton2020
    description: Examines economic, environmental, and social impacts of electric vehicle adoption for public transportation in the Philippines, identifying barriers and policy recommendations.
    epistemic_model: Technology adoption and impact assessment (economic, environmental, social dimensions)
    method: Case study analysis (Philippines)
END SOURCE

ITEM @agaton2020
    text: Despite the benefits of reducing the dependence on fossil fuels and greenhouse gas emissions, the adoption of electric vehicles faces several obstacles ranging from financing issues, government policies, and public acceptance.

    note: *complex* Three-factor convergence (financing, policy, acceptance) reveals adoption requires integrated intervention across economic, regulatory, and social domains

    chain: Financing -> CONSTRAINS -> Adoption

    note: Policy gaps constrain technology adoption by failing to provide necessary regulatory framework and support mechanisms

    chain: Policy -> CONSTRAINS -> Adoption

    note: Public acceptance acts as social constraint on technology adoption independent of technical or economic factors

    chain: Acceptance -> CONSTRAINS -> Adoption
END ITEM

ITEM @agaton2020
    text: This study recommends stricter implementation of government policies on modernized public transportation, stronger government support on financing mechanisms, establishment of charging stations in public and private terminals, and boosting programs for developing local-made electric vehicles.

    note: Policy implementation enables financing mechanisms which enable infrastructure deployment, revealing sequential pathway for technology adoption

    chain: Policy -> ENABLES -> Financing -> ENABLES -> Infrastructure
END ITEM

ITEM @agaton2020
    text: To make electric vehicle more environment-friendly, the government must accelerate the energy transition by increasing the electricity share from renewable sources and investing in more sustainable sources of energy.

    note: Energy transition enables environmental benefits by addressing upstream electricity generation, revealing systemic dependency beyond vehicle technology

    chain: Energy_Transition -> ENABLES -> Environment
END ITEM

SOURCE @abdin2024
    description: Comprehensive review of hydrogen co-firing with natural gas examining technical benefits, challenges, economic implications, and societal acceptance for sustainable energy transition.
    epistemic_model: Socio-technical systems analysis integrating technological, economic, regulatory, and social dimensions of energy transition
    method: Literature review with global case studies
END SOURCE

ITEM @abdin2024
    text: It also provides a critical examination of the economic implications and societal acceptance, which are crucial for the adoption of hydrogen technologies.

    note: Economic viability and social acceptance function as dual necessary conditions for technology adoption, revealing parallel prerequisite pathways.

    chain: Economic_Viability -> ENABLES -> Adoption

    note: Social acceptance operates as independent necessary condition alongside economic factors for technology adoption.

    chain: Acceptance -> ENABLES -> Adoption
END ITEM

ITEM @abdin2024
    text: Ultimately, this comprehensive analysis underscores the essential role of hydrogen co-firing in the energy sector and advocates for a comprehensive strategy that synergizes technological progress, economic viability, regulatory support, and public engagement to promote a resilient and sustainable energy future.

    note: *complex* Four-factor convergence (technology, economic viability, regulation, engagement) reveals integrated multi-domain strategy requirement for sustainable energy transition.

    chain: Technology -> ENABLES -> Energy_Transition

    note: Economic viability operates as independent enabling pathway within multi-factor convergence for energy transition.

    chain: Economic_Viability -> ENABLES -> Energy_Transition

    note: Regulatory support functions as independent enabling mechanism within integrated strategy for energy transition.

    chain: Regulation -> ENABLES -> Energy_Transition

    note: Public engagement operates as independent enabling pathway within multi-factor convergence for energy transition.

    chain: Engagement -> ENABLES -> Energy_Transition
END ITEM

SOURCE @addassi1998
    description: Development of in-situ burning policy for oil spill response in California marine environments through stakeholder coordination and consensus-building.
    epistemic_model: Policy development and stakeholder engagement theory
    method: Policy development process description (no empirical data collection mentioned)
END SOURCE

ITEM @addassi1998
    text: This paper outlines the current efforts being spearheaded by the Office of Oil Spill Prevention and Response (OSPR) in the California Department of Fish and Game, to develop an in-situ burning policy to address oil spills in the open-water marine environments of the State. It describes the importance of early coordination and cooperation amongst the stakeholders in an in-situ burning decision, the formation of a working-group, and a discussion of the consensus approach integral to current policy development efforts.

    note: Reveals mechanism: stakeholder coordination enables consensus-building which enables policy development for controversial technology

    chain: Stakeholder_Coordination -> ENABLES -> Consensus -> ENABLES -> Policy_Development
END ITEM

ITEM @addassi1998
    text: The use of this technology, however, often requires the approval of many different oversight agencies and its use has often been very controversial.

    note: Multiple agency approval requirements constrain technology deployment by creating institutional barriers to implementation

    chain: Regulation -> CONSTRAINS -> Technology_Deployment
END ITEM

ITEM @addassi1998
    text: The implications of ``negative public perception'' regarding the use of in-situ burning will be discussed and future plans for a public outreach and education program will be outlined.

    note: Negative perception contested-by education program reveals mechanism: information provision as strategy to overcome opposition

    chain: Public_Perception -> CONTESTED-BY -> Education_Program
END ITEM
